This was by far the furthest I've gotten in Nethack.  I'd never even
gotten past the quest before, and now here I was, already three levels
into Gehennom, and feeling like this time I was going to finally
ascend.
I was playing Blip, a dwarvish Valkyrie. Her first big break came
very early in the game: a magic lamp from Minetown yielded me gray
dragon scale mail.  The RGN provided me with lots of alters all
throughout the mines, so I was soon carrying around a +4 Mojo.  I
wished for Gauntlets of Power from the Castle WoW to round out the
Valk/Mojo combo (but used my only charge without remembering to order
scrolls of charging).  I now hit really hard, had a great ranged
attack, and an AC of -21, with all fooproof armor.  Through wraith
corpses and nurse dancing I was plvl23 and had about 350hp.
I probably should have forseen my downfall when I made a couple
blunders in Gehennom.  For whatever accidental reason, I didn't have my
helmet on when I approached a mind flayer.  I was able to get it on
just in time to not die of brainlessness, but half of my item
identifications had disappeared.  OK, this was a big hassle, I thought,
but not the end of the world.  I had picked up a spellbook of identify
somewhere along my journey, and with my helmet and gauntlets off, it
only had a 52% fail rate.  I had plenty of food, so I decided to sit
around until I had all my inventory identified again.  The hardest part
was waiting around for my magic power to recover, which took a really
long time as a Valk.  Just so I could keep track of what monsters were
accumulating around the dungeon while I waited, I blinded myself with a
towel and let telepathy take over.  Eventually a cockatrice scurried
over and I did it in quickly with my Mojo, no problem.  But with my
gauntlets off and still blinded, I stupidly walked over the corpse a
few turns later and stoned myself.  My heart nearly stopped.  DYWYPI?
Nope. Not this time.  Your AoLS disintegrates.  Phew.  I forgot I was
even wearing that thing.  At this point, I decided it would be best to
save for the night.
I resumed the next night thinking I'd be a little more cautious.
While battling some sort of heat monster, I noticed that I'd somehow
lost fire resistance.  I made note of that as I continued.  I killed a
black dragon and ate it for disintegration resistance, which must have
satiated me.  A few turns later, a quivering blog came along, and
thinking I'd have a chance to get fire resistance back, I scarfed it
down.
Replies saying that quivering blob corpses don't convey fire
resistance will NOT be viewed as consolation.
